==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Young Man Mola''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Citizen Mola''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Baby Mola/Young Man Mola is effectively a glass cannon version of ([[Crazed Lizard Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Manic]]) [[Lizard Cat (Normal Cat)|King Dragon Cat]]. While its health is almost zero and attack power/range aren't on Manic King Dragon's level, its fast attack rate allows a Baby Mola at Lvl.30 nearly match the DPS of a Lvl.20+80 King Dragon and exceed that of a Lvl.30 Manic King Dragon. In addition, its recharge rate is ~15% faster, though Dragon Cat recharges faster, and the cost is nearly 1/4th lower, allowing Young Man Mola to be stacked more quickly and cheaply. In any stage where the enemies cannot hit them consistently, they are a viable replacement for the Dragons.

==='''True Form'''===
Citizen Mola has their recharge time improved to the point that even Dragon Cat cannot keep up, allowing for even faster stacking. Unfortunately, Citizen Mola retains the extreme fragility of their previous forms, so they are still limited in use to stages where the player is confident they won't be immediately crushed. Such stages being those with an absence of [[:Category:Wave Attack Enemies|waves]], [[:Category:Long Distance Enemies|Long Distance enemies]], or [[:Category:Zombie Enemies|Zombie]] enemies.

==='''Talents'''===
These Talents may look bizarre, and that's because they are. No stat buffs this time around, it's all crowd-control that turns them into a spammable Good luck Ebisu; each Talent is identical in activation rate, uptime, and NP cost, save for Knockback. Fully investing in Citizen Mola's talents turns them into a debuffing monster, especially if stacked as enemies simply cannot advance due to the constant debuffs applied. Their priority entirely depends on the innate value of each ability when compared to one another, as well as their synergies. In order of performance:
#If you want to seriously invest into Citizen Mola, '''[[Freeze]]''' should be your first priority because of just how comically powerful the ability to Freeze all traits is on such a spammable backliner. Any stage with enemies that don't outrange Citizen Mola can be swept by this Talent alone if you play your cards right. Getting a good stack of Citizen Molas set up essentially means CC-hell for even the most powerful melees/midrangers.
#'''[[Freeze|Slow]]''' is worse than Freeze in most aspects, but pairs exceptionally well with it. If an enemy isn't procced with Freeze, it can still get Slowed, and both abilities hold an enemy in place until Citizen Mola's next attack cycle. It's basically crowd-control insurance, making it way easier to stack him.
#One of Citizen Mola's biggest limiting factors is their Single Attack, which can make it difficult for them to shake off large hordes of tanky enemies. '''[[Knockback]]''' attenuates this issue by allowing your stack the ability to protect itself. A good stack of Citizen Molas is more than capable of keeping entire armies at bay, especially when paired with Freeze and Slow. Do note that this Talent can make it difficult to use him with other units.
#'''[[Weaken]]''' is a third layer of defense that specifically protects your meatshields instead of holding threats in place like Slow and Freeze. If you don't wish to unlock Knockback, Weaken can be a good alternative to preserve potential synergies with other cats. The ability comes with its own set of advantages against strong bosses who only need a single breakthrough to cause a mass extinction event; so, even if nothing else activates, the threat will still have to make it past your meatshields and tanks while Weakened.
#Finally, '''[[Curse]]''' is situational at the best of times, and only helps when enemies rely primarily on abilities to push, like [[M. Ost]] or [[Star Peng]]. Your other Talents will usually cover that front well enough, though, and the option to use appropriate meatshields for the occasion is always there. The NP sink is just not worth it a lot of the time.

==='''Orb'''===
Due to Citizen Mola's low HP, Defense Orbs do practically nothing. If you want to target a specific trait, an S grade Attack Orb will increase Citizen Mola's damage by ~18.5% at Lv 50.

For those willing to investing in Citizen Mola, the Cost Down Orb works especially well on them, with their low cooldown (especially with research combos), and somewhat costly price.
As a bonus, the opportunity cost of using Cost Down on Citizen Mola is relatively low, as every meatshield would rather have the Cash Back Orb, and most other units have ready made Effect Orbs, or are too cost effective to be worth putting it on. If you don't plan on using it for specifically Cyborg, consider equiping Citizen Mola with the Cost Down Orb.

